@ericstj There is another issue that shows our source generated validators, which are using attributes mentioned in this issue:
https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/issues/85475

It would be also helpful to create an X509Certificate2 validation attribute. We have it in our backlog but unfortunately had no capacity to create it.

I held an offline conversation with @ajcvickers regarding the concerns that he expressed in https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/issues/77402#issuecomment-1291737278, namely the potential for extensions in existing attributes to break downstream consumers that provide their own validation implementations. We think that even though consumers would need to be updated so that the new configuration can be recognized, we don't believe this should manifest as a breaking change for annotations using the current configuration space. 

This means that we could add `ExclusiveRangeAttribute` functionality by exposing two new properties to the existing `RangeAttribute`:

```diff
public partial class RangeAttribute
{
+    public bool IsLowerBoundInclusive { get; set; } = true;
+    public bool IsUpperBoundInclusive { get; set; } = true;
}
```

Similarly, `NonDefaultValueRequiredAttribute` could be exposed as a property in `RequiredAttribute`:

```diff
public partial class RequiredAttribute
{
+    public bool DisallowDefaultValues { get; set; } = false;
}
```

How about using just one ctor like so?
```C#
public LengthAttribute(int minLength = 0, int maxLength = -1)
{
      MinLength = minLength;
      MaxLength = maxLength < 0 ? null : maxLength;
}
```
